  "question": "Can the legacy CNKI static-index identifier pattern be safely used to enumerate the exact 1951 issue-9 Zhao Wanli article from the current public web without bypassing TLS verification, and what does a failed enumeration mean?",
  "answer": "No target enumeration is currently valid. A public Wikisource discussion page supplies only a secondary discovery control showing that another 1951 《文物参考资料》 article is associated with legacy CNKI code WENW195106001. The first probe extrapolated hypothetical WENW195109001-040 URLs but every request failed before content retrieval because www.cnki.com.cn presented a certificate whose names did not match that host. A second calibrated probe tested the known-positive control WENW195106001 across four safe public host/scheme variants without disabling TLS verification: HTTPS www failed certificate hostname validation; HTTPS apex timed out; HTTP www returned an empty HTTP 418 response; HTTP apex timed out. No route passed the known-positive control, so the second probe deliberately enumerated zero target candidates. Therefore this closes only a current public transport/access boundary. It does not establish any target CNKI identifier, does not show the article is absent from CNKI, and contributes zero direct article text.",
